// Test 26: A busy divide survives a self-modifying-code squash
// klaussCPU 9ad6614 fixed this; it is the hardware session's board test.
// A store that writes the code doubleword holding the instruction in ID
// squashes ID and refetches it (the SMC squash). Cores before 9ad6614 also
// cleared EX, so a multicycle op still busy there (DIV/MUL, DELAY, an LCD
// wait) was dropped and never re-executed: G kept 0x55. 9ad6614 clears EX
// only when EX itself is squashed or has advanced.
//
// The MEMSET64 rewrites, with the same bytes, the doubleword that starts at
// NEXT, while the DIVR in the doubleword before it is busy in EX. That needs
// NEXT to be 8-byte aligned with the DIVR directly before it; corpus_tests.rs
// checks this, because the emulator does not model the pipeline and passes
// either way. An instruction added or removed above PAD moves NEXT.
//
// Expected UART output:
//   0000008E  (1000 / 7)

_start
SETR P 0xFFFFF
COPY O P
SETR C 1000
SETR D 7
SETR G 0x55
SETR B NEXT:
MEMGET64 E B        // E = the code doubleword holding NEXT (8-byte aligned load)
NOP
NOP
NOP
NOP
PAD:
MEMSET64 E B        // rewrite that doubleword with the same bytes: SMC squash of ID
DIVR G C D          // busy in EX when the store completes
NEXT:
NOP                 // must start a new doubleword (NEXT % 8 == 0)
$TXR G
$NEWLINE
HALT

!include uart_stubs.kla
